Given circuit is shown below, <img src="img/821d260e0dbffa87.webp" loading="lazy" alt="" style="width:min(100%,416px)"> The above circuit is a practical differentiator. The transfer function is given by, \[\frac{V_{out}(s)}{V_{in}(s)} = \frac{-Z_2(s)}{Z_1(s)} = \frac{-R_2}{R_1 + \frac{1}{C_1s}} = \frac{-sC_1R_2}{1 + sC_1R_1}\] \[T(s) = \left| \frac{V_{out}(s)}{V_{in}(s)} \right| = \frac{Z_2(s)}{Z_1(s)} = \frac{sC_1R_2}{1 + sC_1R_1}\] Put \(s = j\omega\), \[T(j\omega) = \frac{j\omega C_1R_2}{1 + j\omega C_1R_1}\] \[\text{At } \omega = 0, T(0) = \frac{0}{1+0}=0\] \[\text{At } \omega = \infty, T(\infty) = \frac{jC_1R_2}{\frac{1}{\infty}+jC_1R_1} = \frac{R_2}{R_1}\] So, circuit behave as HPF, because it passes only high frequency and block low frequency. The \(3\text{ dB}\) frequency of practical differentiator circuit is, \[\omega_{3\text{dB}} = \frac{1}{R_1C_1} = \frac{1}{1000 \times 1\,\mu\text{F}} = 1000\,\text{rad/sec}\] \[\boxed{\begin{array}{l}
\text{(B)}\quad \text{The circuit is a high pass filter.}\\
\text{(C)}\quad \text{The 3 dB frequency is }1000\ \text{rad/s.}
\end{array}}\] <div class="sol-block sol-keypoints"><div class="sol-block-title">Key points</div><ul><li><strong>Practical Differentiator:</strong> The addition of \(R_1\) in series with the input capacitor \(C_1\) limits the high-frequency gain, effectively transforming the ideal differentiator into a high-pass filter.</li></ul></div>

